WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.940 --> 00:00:04.830
Turns out, the man most famous for playing
the wizarding world's nastiest teacher was

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:08.640
also a dedicated friend, a mentor, and an
enormous amount of fun to be around.

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:09.660
Who’d have guessed it?

00:00:10.180 --> 00:00:14.480
Alan Rickman might be gone, but he left us
a lot to think about and a lot to take as

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:15.120
inspiration.

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:19.260
In January 2016, Rickman died of pancreatic
cancer.

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:23.200
At 69, he was no spring chicken but he wasn’t
particularly old, either.

00:00:23.440 --> 00:00:27.960
Fans around the world mourned both the untimely
nature of his passing and its seeming suddenness.

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:29.400
Had anyone known he was sick?

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:32.140
How long had he been suffering, and how severely?

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:33.660
Had it been publicized?

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:38.710
How could one beloved man's passing, in an
age of such unprecedented interconnectivity,

00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:40.420
so shock the entire world?

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:42.500
Simply put: it was a secret.

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:46.699
The first new thing the world learned about
Alan Rickman upon his death was that he had

00:00:46.699 --> 00:00:51.210
been battling pancreatic cancer for a few
months prior, after being diagnosed in August

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:52.280
following a small stroke.

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:57.580
Pancreatic cancer, sometimes called a "silent
cancer," takes time to present any symptoms,

00:00:57.590 --> 00:01:01.249
and is often found after a seemingly unconnected
problem surfaces.

00:01:01.249 --> 00:01:04.449
Rickman decided to keep the news confined
to close friends and family, and soldiered

00:01:04.449 --> 00:01:05.280
on with his work.

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:09.300
As it happened, no one knew what they were
going to lose until he was already gone.

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:14.760
Though Rickman portrayed many noteworthy characters,
Severus Snape Harry Potter's grouchy potions

00:01:14.760 --> 00:01:17.000
professor might be the most memorable of all.

00:01:17.240 --> 00:01:22.680
Snape is vindictive, moody, and even in his
moments of bravery, deeply and unrelentingly

00:01:22.680 --> 00:01:23.180
cynical.

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:26.440
It's a fine edge to dance upon, but Rickman
made it look easy.

00:01:26.720 --> 00:01:30.880
For eight films, he portrayed Snape with caustic
aplomb, and that performance has become nothing

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:31.880
less than iconic.

00:01:32.080 --> 00:01:35.480
“That is the second time you’ve spoken
out of turn Ms. Granger are you incapable

00:01:35.480 --> 00:01:40.060
of restraining yourself or do you take pride
in being an insufferable know it all?”

00:01:40.780 --> 00:01:41.900
“He’s got a point, you know.”

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:46.040
By all accounts, it was also the role that
had the least to do with his actual personality.

00:01:46.050 --> 00:01:50.440
When remembering the actor posthumously, colleagues
and friends have repeatedly touched upon his

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:52.980
unrelenting kindness and generosity of spirit.

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:56.040
In a tribute for Entertainment Weekly, Kate
Winslet mused:

00:01:56.180 --> 00:02:00.380
"If there was one word I could use to really
describe Al, it would be kind.

00:02:00.640 --> 00:02:05.360
He was so kind… whenever Al would go out
to supper and anyone else would try and pay,

00:02:05.360 --> 00:02:09.119
he would somehow have phoned ahead or slipped
his credit card ahead of the meal so no one

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:10.440
even got a look at the check."

00:02:10.780 --> 00:02:15.010
Tales of helping hands, thoughtful gifts,
and general friendliness seem to surround

00:02:15.010 --> 00:02:15.640
Rickman’s legacy.

00:02:15.880 --> 00:02:20.060
He wasn't just passively kind but actively
generous which is about as far from Snape

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:20.820
as you can get.

00:02:21.020 --> 00:02:25.400
Younger fans will never know how heated the
debate over Snape's true loyalties grew back

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:28.100
when the Harry Potter saga was still a work
in progress.

00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:32.780
Snape's motivations were murky and his actions
ambiguous until the very end, when it was

00:02:32.780 --> 00:02:37.140
finally revealed that he was on the side of
the angels all along, motivated by his deep

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:39.380
and lasting love for Harry's mother Lily.

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:44.980
Though some had guessed, no one knew the full
truth for sure until the story’s very final

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:45.480
chapters.

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:47.340
No one, that is, except Alan Rickman.

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:49.660
It's since come to light that author J.K.

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:54.660
Rowling told Rickman the truth about Snape's
motivations years earlier to help inform his

00:02:54.660 --> 00:02:55.360
performance.

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:58.760
According to Rowling, what she told him boiled
down to just one word:

00:03:00.420 --> 00:03:01.320
“Always.”

00:03:02.620 --> 00:03:07.060
If you revisit the Potter films, this knowledge
will become more readily apparent in Rickman's

00:03:07.060 --> 00:03:11.959
performance: although he is sour, unrelentingly
harsh, and sometimes even outright cruel,

00:03:11.960 --> 00:03:15.220
he returns, over and over again, to the side
of light and love.

00:03:15.820 --> 00:03:19.840
His anger at Harry appears so much more nuanced
than at first glance, and his frustration

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:22.080
with the growing darkness around him more
pronounced.

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:26.480
Snape was always much more than he seemed,
and Rickman made that apparent in everything

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:31.100
from overt declarations of loyalty to the
subtlest, most fleeting glances.

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:35.960
Though Rickman's trademark style and double-bass
voice made every role he took on memorable

00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:41.460
in its own right, arguably one of his best
was Alexander Dane in 1999’s Galaxy Quest.

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:46.500
Also an actor, the character of Dane feels
degraded by his role as Dr. Lazarus on the

00:03:46.520 --> 00:03:51.200
in-universe Star-Trek-equivalent Galaxy Quest,
which requires the use of a complicated and

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:55.060
silly-looking skull prosthetic as well as
a horde of alien catchphrases.

00:03:56.440 --> 00:04:03.180
“By Grabthar’s hammer… what a savings.”

00:04:04.360 --> 00:04:08.780
Galaxy Quest became an instant cult hit, and
is often hailed in Star Trek fandom as a better

00:04:08.780 --> 00:04:10.820
Trek movie than some of the official outings.

00:04:11.060 --> 00:04:15.020
Rickman's performance in particular, of course,
is held to be one of the film's most memorable.

00:04:15.380 --> 00:04:19.070
So it’s probably no surprise to find that
a sequel was in the works, which was kept

00:04:19.070 --> 00:04:23.000
totally under wraps until Rickman’s tragic
passing put an end to the project.

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:27.080
It's a bittersweet turn of events that acts
as both a testament to Rickman's acting and

00:04:27.080 --> 00:04:28.880
a sad reminder of what could have been.

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:33.760
There are few relationships in the Harry Potter
series more charged and complex than the one

00:04:33.760 --> 00:04:35.160
between Harry and Snape.

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:39.020
After all, Harry Potter and the Half-Blood
Prince pretty much translates as "Harry Potter

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:40.060
and Severus Snape”.

00:04:40.340 --> 00:04:44.700
Snape basically gives his life to protect
Harry, yet bullies him relentlessly for years.

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:49.440
Meanwhile, Harry loathes Snape to the point
of obsession yet ends up naming a son after

00:04:49.440 --> 00:04:49.940
him.

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:53.560
Their relationship sits at the heart of the
series and the crux of Harry’s journey.

00:04:53.900 --> 00:04:57.580
It's only appropriate, then, that Alan Rickman
matters so deeply to Daniel Radcliffe.

00:04:57.740 --> 00:05:01.720
In a Facebook post made after Rickman’s
death, Radcliffe hailed him as one of the

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:03.580
greatest actors he would ever work with.

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:04.520
He continued:

00:05:04.540 --> 00:05:08.320
“He is also one of the loyalest and most
supportive people I've ever met in the film

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:08.820
industry."

00:05:09.220 --> 00:05:13.660
Radcliffe went on to detail Rickman's kindness
and generosity, his mastery of the craft of

00:05:13.660 --> 00:05:17.420
acting, and his wit, adding one particularly
heart-wrenching detail:

00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:21.480
"I'm pretty sure he came and saw everything
I ever did on stage both in London and New

00:05:21.480 --> 00:05:21.980
York."

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:27.220
Rickman's dedication to performance was legendary
but it was easily surpassed by his devotion

00:05:27.220 --> 00:05:28.540
to his fellow performers.

